<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Binnya U</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1323">1323</a>–<a href="/years/1384">1384</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Binnya U was king of the Mon kingdom of Martaban–Hanthawaddy in lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1348 to 1384. His long reign was troubled by rebellions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and attacks from neighbouring states, and in 1369 he moved the capital from Martaban to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>. He was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/razadarit">Razadarit</a>, who restored the kingdom's strength and unity.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born around late 1323, Binnya U was the only son of King Saw Zein and Queen Sanda Min Hla of Martaban, and a grandnephew of the dynasty's founder, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wareru">Wareru</a>. His father's assassination in 1330 ended his place as heir, but his mother, a feared kingmaker, put her half-brother Binnya E Law on the throne. In the 1340s U defeated E Law's son Binnya E Laung in an elephant-back duel; E Laung died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> soon after, and when E Law died in 1348 U succeeded at 25, taking the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hsinbyushin">Hsinbyushin</a>, Lord of the White Elephant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He bought the court's loyalty with marriage alliances. In the dry season of 1351–52 an army of 8,000 from Lan Na, invited by the rebel ruler of Donwun, overran northern Martaban; U led the counterattack on his white elephant and drove it back, and in 1352–53 he retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> from his half-brother Min Linka, who was executed. A decade of peace followed, in which he governed with Chief Minister Pun So and in 1362 raised the Shwedagon Pagoda to 20 metres.</p><p>About February 1364, while he was away hunting for a new white elephant, the princes Byattaba and Laukpya seized the capital. U held out at the dynasty's ancestral home of Donwun until 1369, when after Pun So's death 700 of Byattaba's warriors disguised as mourners took his palace and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>. The province was central and the most populous, and the kingdom came to be called Hanthawaddy after Pegu's classical name. In 1370–71 U signed a border treaty with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swa-saw-ke">Swa Saw Ke</a> of Ava, bought a truce from the rebel brothers with gold and elephants, and in 1375–76 backed Toungoo against Ava in a proxy war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ill since at least 1364, he left more and more of the government to his elder sister Maha Dewi, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagon</a>. He had chosen his younger son Baw Ngan-Mohn as heir, and on 5 May 1383 his eldest son Binnya Nwe, raised by Maha Dewi, rebelled and seized Dagon. In October U formally handed power to his sister, but the court under Chief Minister Zeik-Bye sided with Nwe, whose small army reached the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> on 10 December. U died on 2 January 1384, and two days later the court gave power to Nwe. Pegu remained the capital of the Mon-speaking kingdom until the mid-16th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Binnya U” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His dynasty united the Mon-speaking coast of Ramannadesa, and in 1369 King <b>Binnya U</b> moved the capital to Pegu, also called Hanthawaddy.</blockquote><a href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mottama, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Bago, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.53,97.6&amp;year=1362" aria-label="Mottama on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7207 1549 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Binnya U"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7657 1819h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7657 1819h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=16.53,97.6&amp;year=1362">What was here in 1362? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
